scripts/check-changelog.sh:1:#!/usr/bin/env bash
scripts/check-changelog.sh:2:# Fails when a diff touches src/ and leaves CHANGELOG.md alone.
scripts/check-changelog.sh:3:#
scripts/check-changelog.sh:4:# Usage:
scripts/check-changelog.sh:5:#   bash scripts/check-changelog.sh [base-ref]     # default: origin/main
scripts/check-changelog.sh:6:#
scripts/check-changelog.sh:7:# The rule is about what a consumer can observe, not about effort: anything that
scripts/check-changelog.sh:8:# changes shipped behaviour needs a sentence they can read on npm. A change to
scripts/check-changelog.sh:9:# src/ that genuinely is not user-visible - an internal refactor, a test-only
scripts/check-changelog.sh:10:# edit - takes the `no-changelog` label instead, which is a deliberate, visible
scripts/check-changelog.sh:11:# act rather than a silent omission.
scripts/check-changelog.sh:16:# A base ref that does not resolve means nothing was compared. On a laptop that
scripts/check-changelog.sh:17:# is ordinary - a fresh clone has no origin/main - and skipping is right. In CI
scripts/check-changelog.sh:18:# it is the gate passing without running, which is the one outcome a gate must
scripts/check-changelog.sh:19:# never have, and it is exactly how a fetch that failed went unnoticed.
