scripts/smoke.sh:2:# Packs the package, installs the tarball into a temp directory OUTSIDE this
scripts/smoke.sh:3:# repository, and runs smoke/ against it.
scripts/smoke.sh:4:#
scripts/smoke.sh:5:# Usage:
scripts/smoke.sh:6:#   bash scripts/smoke.sh            # both variants
scripts/smoke.sh:7:#   bash scripts/smoke.sh bare       # no optional peer installed
scripts/smoke.sh:8:#   bash scripts/smoke.sh full       # both optional peers installed
scripts/smoke.sh:9:#
scripts/smoke.sh:10:# Outside the tree on purpose: installed inside it, Node and tsc walk up to this
scripts/smoke.sh:11:# repo's own node_modules and every missing dependency resolves anyway, so the
scripts/smoke.sh:12:# check passes on a package that would fail for a real consumer.
scripts/smoke.sh:13:#
scripts/smoke.sh:14:# Two variants because a consumer is in one of two states, and they fail
scripts/smoke.sh:15:# differently: `bare` is a mock-provider consumer, where an absent peer must fail
scripts/smoke.sh:16:# at construction with a named error; `full` is a Bedrock or Vertex consumer,
scripts/smoke.sh:17:# where the SDK must actually load.
scripts/smoke.sh:39:# A consumer directory inside the repository would resolve this repo's node_modules by walking up,
scripts/smoke.sh:40:# which is the one thing this check exists to rule out.
scripts/smoke.sh:51:# The process must end on its own. A smoke test that needs killing is hiding a leaked handle from
scripts/smoke.sh:52:# every consumer, and the engine's own heartbeat timer was once exactly that.
